    "model": "The line graph compares the unemployment rate, given as a percentage, in two regions—the North and the South—across a ten-year period between 2010 and 2020.\n\nOverall, the two regions followed sharply contrasting trajectories: unemployment in the North fell steadily throughout the decade, whereas in the South it rose just as consistently. Consequently, the regions had effectively reversed their positions by 2020, with their lines intersecting midway through the period.\n\nAt the start, the North posted by far the higher figure, at 8.5%, almost three times the South's 3%. Thereafter, the northern rate dropped without interruption, easing to 7.2% in 2012 and 6% in 2014, before sliding on to 5.1% in 2016, 4.3% in 2018 and a low of 3.8% in 2020—an overall fall of 4.7 percentage points.\n\nThe South, by contrast, saw unemployment creep upwards from its initial low, climbing to 4.1% and 5.5% in the first four years. The two lines crossed between 2014 and 2016, and the southern rate then continued to rise, reaching 7% in 2018 and peaking at 7.6% in 2020, by which point it was double the northern figure.",
