  "title": "Public Services vs Arts",
  "prompt": "Governments should spend more money on essential public services such as health and education than on supporting the arts. To what extent do you agree or disagree?\n\nGive reasons for your answer and include relevant examples. Write at least 250 words.",
  "visual": null,
  "model": "It is sometimes argued that public money should be directed towards vital sectors like healthcare and education rather than towards cultural and artistic endeavours. While I acknowledge that the arts enrich society, I largely agree that governments ought to prioritise essential services, since these directly determine citizens' welfare and future prospects.\n\nThe most compelling reason for favouring health and education is that they underpin the very survival and productivity of a nation. Without accessible hospitals, preventable illnesses can devastate families, while a poorly funded school system leaves young people ill-equipped for employment. In Indonesia, for instance, sustained investment in the national health insurance scheme, BPJS, has allowed millions of low-income patients to receive treatment they could never otherwise afford, demonstrating how public spending on medicine translates into tangible, life-saving outcomes that no gallery or theatre could match.\n\nNevertheless, I would stop short of arguing that cultural funding should be eliminated, because the arts also yield meaningful social and economic benefits. Museums, festivals and film industries generate employment, attract tourism and strengthen national identity. The success of South Korea illustrates this point: government support for its creative sector helped turn music and cinema into a global export worth billions of dollars. The wiser approach, therefore, is not to abandon the arts but to fund them modestly, ensuring that limited resources are weighted heavily towards services on which lives depend.\n\nIn conclusion, although artistic pursuits certainly deserve a place in national budgets, I firmly believe that health and education must take precedence, as they ultimately safeguard the wellbeing and opportunities of every citizen.",
